WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:07.200
Imagine a version of Earth fifty million years&nbsp;
in the future — one where humanity has vanished,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:13.600
and extraordinary new creatures now populate the&nbsp;
planet. This hypothetical future is the topic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.920
the retro speculative zoology book ‘After Man,’&nbsp;
written by a founder of the genre — Dougal Dixon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.240
The book is part of a trilogy alongside ‘Man After&nbsp;
Man’ and ‘The New Dinosaurs’ — ( I have a video on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:32.480
The new Dinosaurs in the archive). But ‘After Man’&nbsp;
is Dixon’s original masterwork, and an influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:38.320
behind other series like the Future is Wild,&nbsp;
and C. M. Kosemen's infamous ‘All Tomorrows.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.400
Its impact no surprise, as the post-human Earth&nbsp;
Dixon brings to life within the pages feels so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:51.360
familiar, and yet so alien. And the biology of the&nbsp;
futuristic animals he showcases are so strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.560
it’s worth taking a closer look. So, for this&nbsp;
entry into the archive, we’re going to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.600
down the animal ecosystems of the far future,&nbsp;
bearing in mind that — as I mentioned in my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.960
New Dinosaur’s video — some of the science is&nbsp;
outdated, and it’s impossible to know exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:13.040
what such a world would look like. Think of this&nbsp;
book and this video as just an interesting guess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.120
So, let’s leap forwards fifty million years, and&nbsp;
discover what types of fantastical species might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.880
now reign over the planet. Beginning in the&nbsp;
vast temperate forests and grasslands of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:31.120
reborn Earth, the planet’s biome distribution has&nbsp;
shifted dramatically. 50 million years from now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:37.360
tectonic movements will likely rearrange the&nbsp;
continents, pushing Africa into Eurasia, Eurasia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:43.600
into North America, and separating North and South&nbsp;
America. In these new transcontinental regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:50.080
Dixon imagines you can find various species of&nbsp;
rabbucks — sizeable, swift-footed grazers with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.760
strange appearances. As you can imagine, rabbucks&nbsp;
are descended from modern day rabbits, but have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.080
become much larger with longer legs. This is&nbsp;
because the rabbucks now occupy the evolutionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:07.920
grazing niche once held by deer, which have&nbsp;
declined in this hypothetical future. The rabbucks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.360
are preyed upon by the Falanx, a mid-sized&nbsp;
predator species with scissor-like molars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.120
These carnivores are roughly wolf-sized. Despite&nbsp;
this, however, the Falanx aren’t the descendants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:24.640
of wolves at all — but a very distant offshoot&nbsp;
of none other than the rat. And I will say:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.440
I’m certainly glad that wolf-sized rats aren’t&nbsp;
something we have to deal with in our time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.800
Moving into the forests, you can find the Oakleaf&nbsp;
Toad: one of my personal favorite new species.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:42.320
These curious amphibians get their name from&nbsp;
the peculiar leaf-shaped growth on their back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.840
with helps with camouflage. Many modern toads&nbsp;
employ camouflage of their own — though few at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.840
the same remarkable level as the oakleaf. Another&nbsp;
way oakleaf toads are different from most modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.520
amphibians is they’re true carnivores — large&nbsp;
enough to eat small mammals instead of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.760
insects. And within the nearby wetlands,&nbsp;
the bizarre Reedstilt lurks: searching for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.360
prey with its sinuous neck and balancing on&nbsp;
its platform-like feet. Despite appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:18.000
the Reedstilt is a descendent of insectivores like&nbsp;
hedgehogs! Over millions of years, these animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.960
lost their quills and became larger and larger&nbsp;
as they transitioned into mid-sized predators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.840
Continuing on to the pine trees of the northern&nbsp;
coniferous forests, we have the hornheads — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:36.480
large animal with unique horns for defense, and&nbsp;
subsist off moss and lichen. These species are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.160
a larger, more defensive incarnation of the&nbsp;
antelope — trading the characteristic speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:47.360
of their ancestors for size and security&nbsp;
from predators. And speaking of predators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:52.320
the Pamthret is one of the most dangerous within&nbsp;
the coniferous forests. These creatures are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.000
descended from ferrets — who are also carnivores&nbsp;
in the modern day, just not quite as dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.320
ones. But even the Pamthrets sometimes think twice&nbsp;
about tangling with this animal — the spine-tailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:09.520
squirrel. As you can tell by the name, they’re&nbsp;
most closely related to modern ground squirrels:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.000
although they’ve taken a page out of the&nbsp;
porcupine’s handbook, and now sport long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:19.600
pointy quills for warding off predators. A&nbsp;
creative, and effective survival strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.320
Moving even further northwards, we arrive at the&nbsp;
windswept tundra and below-zero polar regions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.560
Here, you can find the wooly gigantalope, a thick&nbsp;
furred beast with massive forward-facing horns for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.320
clearing snow off the vegetation it consumes. As&nbsp;
you can likely tell by the name, this species is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.920
another offshoot of the modern antelope, although&nbsp;
its lumbering size and massive horns make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:50.560
in many ways similar to a rhino. In the nearby&nbsp;
icy mountains dwell the sure-footed Gorath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:56.080
a species well equipped for vertical movement&nbsp;
upon the steep slopes. This high-elevation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.880
species is likely the descendent of none other&nbsp;
than the goat, with the horns of the modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:05.760
goat the precursor to the single bony plate&nbsp;
the Gorath uses for territorial displays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.440
And even further north within the icy&nbsp;
waters of what we call the arctic circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:19.040
the leviathan-sized Vortex and smaller Porpin swim&nbsp;
through the cold waters. The vortex is the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:25.760
largest creature on earth, and a clear analogue to&nbsp;
whales, while the porpin shares many similarities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.600
to the modern porpoise or dolphin. Despite this,&nbsp;
these two animals are in fact descended from none&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:38.800
other than penguins — who, over the eons, became&nbsp;
fully aquatic and swelled to immense sizes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.480
We’ve got more fantastical creatures to cover,&nbsp;
including my personal favorite, but since we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.040
halfway through the list, if you’re enjoying this&nbsp;
content, please consider taking a moment to like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.520
subscribe, and hit the notification icon&nbsp;
to stay up to date on all things curious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:01.200
Heading much further south to the equator, we find&nbsp;
a vast sequence of deserts. Here, Dixon imagines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:07.200
the rather unfortunate looking desert leapers roam&nbsp;
the sands. These bulbous-tailed animals might look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.320
(and hop) like kangaroos, but they’re actually&nbsp;
another descendant of modern rodents. The desert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.920
leaper also employs the same unique survival&nbsp;
strategy as the camel — as the creature’s large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:23.680
tail, like the camel’s hump, contains specialized&nbsp;
fat deposits, which allow it to go without food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:29.040
for long periods. Even more wrinkly than&nbsp;
the desert leaper is the desert shark:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.480
an animal with a truly unfortunate appearance.&nbsp;
Their hairless bodies are a carryover from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:40.080
similarly alarming looking ancestors, the naked&nbsp;
mole rats. And like naked mole rats, the sand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.120
sharks (which have no relation to actual sharks)&nbsp;
spend most of their time burrowing underground to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:51.120
regulate body temperature. And the last creature&nbsp;
in the desert is the Rootsucker, a much friendlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.320
looking fellow with hard plates for defense and&nbsp;
long front claws for digging up the roots of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:02.240
succulents it feeds on. The Rootsucker is the&nbsp;
evolutionary legacy of the armadillo, an animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:07.760
that shares its armored shell and also uses its&nbsp;
front claws for digging for food. And are one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.400
of my personal favorite species—though not yet&nbsp;
my very favorite. ontinuing on, also near the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:18.240
equator, a belt of tropical grasslands flourishes,&nbsp;
in a climate similar to what supports elephant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.640
populations today. But instead of elephants, the&nbsp;
lumbering Rundihorn and Southern Gigantelope now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:31.520
dominate the grassy fields. Both species occupy a&nbsp;
similar niche to modern elephants as the heaviest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:37.040
animals on the savanna — and perhaps the&nbsp;
heaviest animals anywhere in this new world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.440
As the name implies, these species are yet another&nbsp;
descendant of the antelope, if you can believe it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.480
But where there are grazers, there are predators,&nbsp;
and the Horrane are the truly bizarre looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:55.840
species which preys on Gigantelope. Despite the&nbsp;
build and coloring of their bodies resembling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.840 --> 00:08:01.760
that of the modern cheetah, as you could probably&nbsp;
tell by their faces the Horrane are descendants of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:07.360
primates, which have become fully carnivorous&nbsp;
ambush predators. And even more horrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:12.480
are the Raboons, another primate offshoot&nbsp;
which became a grassland-dwelling carnivore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:18.160
Descendants of the modern, much less frightening&nbsp;
looking baboon, the Raboons are no longer content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:24.320
to feed on plants and fruit alone, becoming&nbsp;
full-on scavengers. Like their baboon ancestors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:29.120
however, they have close family structures&nbsp;
and live in groups instead of going it alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:36.240
Further south, in the vast tropical forests of&nbsp;
the world, the long-trunked Zarander feed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:42.160
high leafy branches. Despite their trunks,&nbsp;
the Zarander aren’t related to elephants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:48.000
but to modern day wild boars. It’s hard to see the&nbsp;
family resemblance, but fifty million years is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:53.760
lot of time for a species to adapt to a new way&nbsp;
of life. Traveling along to the tropical rivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.040
we’ve got the Swimming Monkey. The name kind of&nbsp;
tells you everything you need to know about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.520
one. They’re a monkey… but they’re good at&nbsp;
swimming. A pretty straightforward creature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.920
And in the same boat, we have the swimming&nbsp;
anteater: which, once again, is an ant-eater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:17.280
but good at swimming. You get it. Much more unique&nbsp;
is the unfortunately named Mud-Gulper — a species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.800
which occupies a similar niche in the swampy&nbsp;
waters as the modern-day hippopotamus, but despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.720
having a very similar looking snout, the mudgulper&nbsp;
is yet another animal descended from a species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:35.920
rodent. And arriving at our last destination, we&nbsp;
have the isolated and diverse world of islands. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:41.680
particular, the enormous island of Lemuria which&nbsp;
broke off from the modern African continent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:48.240
is home to some of the most striking species this&nbsp;
new world has to offer. Meet the Night Stalker:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:55.000
an animal so strange it twists one’s mind just&nbsp;
looking at it. The four-limbed, predatory night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:01.040
stalker is a descendant of the bat, and has given&nbsp;
up the wings of its ancestors in favor of a fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:06.560
terrestrial existence. But the Nightcrawler isn’t&nbsp;
the only descendant of the bat worth mentioning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:13.280
The last species on our list is… this the&nbsp;
Flooer, an absolutely absurd looking animal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:19.760
which resembles a flower in sight and smell to&nbsp;
lure in insects. Crazy though this might seem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:26.320
it’s not actually that far off from how Venus fly&nbsp;
traps catch their prey — although such a trait&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:33.600
evolving in an animal is a truly insane concept.&nbsp;
And for that reason, despite its appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:40.240
it’s my very favorite. And that’s the end of&nbsp;
our voyage 50 million years into the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:46.240
Like I said in my New Dinosaur’s video, Dougal&nbsp;
Dixon’s work, while just an entertaining guess at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:51.920
the future, is worth checking out if you ever get&nbsp;
the chance. n the meantime, thanks for watching.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:58.000
If you liked this video, please lend your support&nbsp;
and like, subscribe, and hit the notification icon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:13.840
if you haven’t already to stay up to date on&nbsp;
all things Curious. See you in the next video.

